{"advisory_context_id":"sha256:f1bcd2aa5282fef80f4e455b29542a2a30c2024df89002b9dcba1996f7a8be1f","attention_pack_id":null,"catalog":{"advisory_context_ref":"sha256:f1bcd2aa5282fef80f4e455b29542a2a30c2024df89002b9dcba1996f7a8be1f","formal_seq":281,"id":"sha256:785c2f84d50a4c6f3d904e656378b88284c9e993cbf67faef0c49afda62593a9","items":[{"excerpt":"[6c4abc40a33f] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: Idempotent and safe are distinct: safe means read-only (no server state change), while idempotent means state may change but identical repeated requests yield the same result as a single one. An append-only event log surviving at-least-once delivery requires writers to be idempotent, not safe, because writers must modify state yet retries must not compound.\nMECHANISM: At-least-once delivery retries writes that may have already succeeded. If the write were merely safe it could not record an event at all (safe = no state mutation). If the write were non-idempotent, each retry would append a duplicate event, corrupting replay determinism. The causal mechanism is deduplication: the writer attaches a stable operation key (or sequence number) so the log's append path can detect a replayed entry and discard it, collapsing the effect of N retries to the e","handle":"A1","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"6c4abc40a33fc6262d6f149079f620d6d42a8edafc3222cc8ce003e69e2b60e8"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 281, artifact 6c4abc40a33fc6262d6f149079f620d6d42a8edafc3222cc8ce003e69e2b60e8 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P1","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:e393d85e31c7dd9dd711ffb2f87d35b2f08a99c519723bcc424115ec81dd57aa"},{"excerpt":"[7e25700ea502] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: Safe is a strictly stronger property than idempotent in the sense that all safe methods are idempotent (repeating a no-op yields a no-op), but the converse fails. An append-only event log needs the weaker property — idempotent writers — because it must tolerate side effects (writing events) while bounding them under retry.\nMECHANISM: RFC 9110 classifies safe methods as idempotent because a read-only operation repeated produces no additional state change. The hierarchy is: safe ⇒ idempotent, but idempotent ⇏ safe (PUT is idempotent but not safe). The log's writers occupy the idempotent-but-not-safe cell: they mutate the log, but their mutation is repeatable. The mechanism is conditional append keyed by a content hash or client-side idempotency token; the log stores a fingerprint of prior writes and rejects duplicates at the append boundary. This is","handle":"A2","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"7e25700ea5020e50f6bb7accdf036b990c66f7155e2c517d545a95d73338006f"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 281, artifact 7e25700ea5020e50f6bb7accdf036b990c66f7155e2c517d545a95d73338006f had formal status accepted.","handle":"P2","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:5d72f2bddd661ba53210a201fadd23b1a56a16765f7fb6cbd2e98a08dca1924f"},{"excerpt":"[03999d039dc1] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: The RFC 9110 distinction is HTTP-semantics-local and does not transfer cleanly to event-log writer semantics. The append-only log does not require 'safe' or 'idempotent' in the RFC sense at all; it requires a stronger, log-specific property: deterministic-replay idempotence, where the replay of a sequence of operations reproduces the same final state regardless of delivery multiplicity, which is neither pure safety nor pure idempotence.\nMECHANISM: RFC 9110 defines idempotence per-method: 'several identical requests have the same effect as one.' But an event log operates on sequences with causality (ordering, dependencies). The required property is that the composition of retried appends is equivalent to a single canonical sequence — a property of the aggregate, not of individual operations. The mechanism is content-addressing or operation-ID dedup","handle":"A3","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"03999d039dc162aaf7537c680eece5767660079c4b661c53d5b5614f720b6556"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 281, artifact 03999d039dc162aaf7537c680eece5767660079c4b661c53d5b5614f720b6556 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P3","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:7a11d6767a4c9ddf2bf87582a1aa1d4fcab2a96f9489a9f82a9b657a072e83e7"},{"excerpt":"[619b760e99d7] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: The append-only event log requires safe operations from its readers and idempotent operations from its writers: the asymmetry is structural. Writers mutate (so not safe) but must be repeatable (idempotent); readers must not mutate (safe) and therefore are trivially idempotent.\nMECHANISM: At-least-once delivery applies to both read and write paths in distributed logs. On the write path, idempotence via deduplication keys is the minimal requirement because safety would forbid writes. On the read path, safety suffices and implies idempotence for free. The causal mechanism is the split of the log protocol into a mutation channel (writer→log, idempotent) and an observation channel (reader←log, safe). This maps to RFC 9110's separation of methods into those that change state (PUT, POST, DELETE) and those that don't (GET, HEAD, OPTIONS).","handle":"A4","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"619b760e99d71edad675228b3450195231353faa8fb263a84b746a2073ca1b68"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 281, artifact 619b760e99d71edad675228b3450195231353faa8fb263a84b746a2073ca1b68 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P4","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:16af3e65c243b44a4cd1b1ace402f340139b17d66948a4055e63aba424b4d2aa"},{"excerpt":"[748e0b32303a] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: Per RFC 9110, a safe method is one whose semantics are essentially read-only (no server state change is intended or expected), while an idempotent method is one where multiple identical requests have the same intended server effect as a single request. The distinction is that safe forbids side effects entirely, whereas idempotent permits side effects but requires them to converge. An append-only event log with at-least-once delivery requires idempotence of its writers — not safety — because appending an event is inherently state-changing (not read-only), yet a retried append must not duplicate the event.\nMECHANISM: Safety constrains the effect class (no mutation). Idempotence constrains the effect's behavior under repetition (mutation is allowed but must be invariant under duplication). In an append-only log, writers mutate state (append), so safe","handle":"A5","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"748e0b32303ad955da49166af6f436a6b8b6f52d58e7550d08cc608cb5100dde"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 281, artifact 748e0b32303ad955da49166af6f436a6b8b6f52d58e7550d08cc608cb5100dde had formal status accepted.","handle":"P5","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:8ab640dbdf573764a92789a02554ce4c7cb66d896dd403ad01053b97f25c09ea"},{"excerpt":"[08240ac1f383] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: The append-only event log does not require idempotence in the RFC 9110 sense at all. RFC 9110 idempotence is a property of HTTP request methods defined over 'intended effect on the server,' which is a semantic, declarative notion. The event log requires operational deduplication — a mechanical guarantee that duplicate physical writes are collapsed — which is a distinct and stronger property. Conflating the two obscures the design problem.\nMECHANISM: RFC 9110 idempotence says 'the intended effect of N identical requests equals the effect of 1.' This is a claim about the method's defined semantics, not a protocol-level mechanism. An event-log writer needs a concrete deduplication mechanism (e.g., monotonically increasing sequence numbers, content-addressed hashing, or client-assigned idempotency keys) to detect and suppress duplicates. This is an op","handle":"A6","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"08240ac1f383f0071c84f068164f72713434b44a727553dbde5fcf445d82ddd5"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 281, artifact 08240ac1f383f0071c84f068164f72713434b44a727553dbde5fcf445d82ddd5 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P6","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:5d48ebbd3e148f66c43b88ed4686113e371862ec7f3ed4ac3cecc166052de8c1"},{"excerpt":"[cded8b977c50] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: RFC 9110 establishes that safe methods are a proper subset of idempotent methods: a safe method produces no side effects, so repeating it trivially yields the same (null) effect, satisfying idempotence. The converse fails: PUT and DELETE are idempotent but not safe. An append-only event log with at-least-once delivery requires idempotence — the weaker, more general property — because writers must mutate state (violating safety) but must not duplicate events under retry (requiring idempotence). Requiring safety would be self-contradictory for a write operation.\nMECHANISM: The subset relation follows from the definitions: safety means 'no intended state change' (RFC 9110 §9.2.1), and idempotence means 'same intended effect for N requests as for 1' (§9.2.2). If the effect of one request is 'no state change,' then the effect of N requests is also 'no ","handle":"A7","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"cded8b977c502922870c0972bda9fcaf7458e11559be202d588bfcd3c98253f3"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 281, artifact cded8b977c502922870c0972bda9fcaf7458e11559be202d588bfcd3c98253f3 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P7","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:55225062dddb6fb7ac556ddcd318bedc7ac3b42592cd6e031d32c92041a46cdf"},{"excerpt":"[8e394f49e7f0] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: The critical distinction is not merely 'safe = read-only, idempotent = convergent under repetition,' but rather that RFC 9110 safety carries a client-expectation dimension: a safe method is one where 'the client does not request, and does not expect, any state change.' Idempotence carries no such expectation — the client expects a state change but expects it to be invariant under duplication. For the event log, this client-expectation dimension is decisive: writers always expect and request a state change (append), so safety is categorically impossible regardless of deduplication strategy. The log requires idempotence because writers expect mutation but must tolerate delivery duplication.\nMECHANISM: RFC 9110 §9.2.1 defines safety as not merely 'no side effect' but as a contract involving client intent: the client does not expect state change. This","handle":"A8","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"8e394f49e7f08af01a5485db4afa96c57bd95d7bd742d77f6a1b22f4c002c8ef"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 281, artifact 8e394f49e7f08af01a5485db4afa96c57bd95d7bd742d77f6a1b22f4c002c8ef had formal status accepted.","handle":"P8","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:32dc8da8f76dda2fafb1d78fb856bdf5c6e478ff300ec26626865c5878f57274"},{"excerpt":"[5d8c3d571ed7] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: An append-only event log actually requires a property stronger than RFC 9110 idempotence: it requires write-ordered idempotence, where not only must duplicate writes be suppressed, but the ordering of non-duplicate writes must be preserved. RFC 9110 idempotence is order-insensitive (PUT A then PUT A then PUT B yields the same state as PUT A then PUT B). But an event log with at-least-once delivery needs order-preserving deduplication: event A, event B, retry of event A must yield [A, B], not [A, B, A]. This ordering constraint is absent from RFC 9110's definition and represents a strictly stronger requirement.\nMECHANISM: RFC 9110 idempotence for PUT says N identical requests yield the same final state as 1 — it says nothing about interleaving with other requests. In an event log, events are ordered, and a retry of an earlier event arriving after l","handle":"A9","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"5d8c3d571ed7d5905c4f583889e38ae5035152dee6b84f0e1841785f089e636b"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 281, artifact 5d8c3d571ed7d5905c4f583889e38ae5035152dee6b84f0e1841785f089e636b had formal status accepted.","handle":"P9","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:260307a2040d0d5fc16d2e7ad540b74dab32838304eed0c1fe957a001755f244"},{"excerpt":"[519095e5cfa6] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: The distinction between safe and idempotent in RFC 9110 is fundamentally about the presence of side effects: safe methods have none (they are read-only operations that do not modify any server resource), while idempotent methods may have side effects but guarantee that the side effects of repeated identical requests equal those of a single request. For an append-only event log, the required property is idempotence. The log cannot require safety because appending is inherently a mutation. But it must require idempotence because at-least-once delivery means the writer's append request may be received and processed multiple times, and the log must remain correct — containing each logical event exactly once regardless of how many times the delivery layer retries.\nMECHANISM: Safe = no side effects (read-only). Idempotent = side effects allowed but inva","handle":"A10","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"519095e5cfa6b4a47054b6b640cd8c977dfbaa5974fddf4c0b4d03f3335f6126"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 281, artifact 519095e5cfa6b4a47054b6b640cd8c977dfbaa5974fddf4c0b4d03f3335f6126 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P10","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:944f239c972e27e40c89fcce49a0a18b8018949d35ced20d328fb3d81d932516"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 281, problem question-4dd976434b71f6e815508e52e995af6b retained an unresolved rivalry among 10 positions.","handle":"P11","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:080b56aa6c46e0a0fd69127948d792855e6ad6e33b8e7af0b547652d1afce56f"},{"excerpt":"Does RFC 9110 define idempotence over the observable response, over server state, or both? This matters: if idempotence is defined over responses only, the event-log deduplication requirement is a separate, stronger property.\nwhy keep: Related advisory scratch: NEW_001\nunfinished: Unresolved question","handle":"B1","kind":"scratch","ref":"sha256:2e9479b9db5b501af8795a4d28bf245155ca2346d244f5e1ae068a08ee680371"},{"excerpt":"Counterfactual: If the event log used a single-writer, single-threaded, no-retry model, idempotence would be unnecessary. But this breaks the at-least-once delivery assumption — the problem explicitly states retries happen. So the necessity of idempotence is conditional on the delivery model, not on the log itself.\nwhy keep: Clarifies that the required property is jointly determined by delivery semantics and operation semantics, not by the log alone.\nunfinished: Could refine to show exactly how at-least-once delivery forces the idempotence requirement.","handle":"B2","kind":"scratch","ref":"sha256:6ea1f4cbb68e5f228c65164b14bcc42117167baeb5dd66de0a68231cb8af6c94"},{"excerpt":"Open question: Does RFC 9110 explicitly state safe ⊂ idempotent, or is the subset relationship only implied? Need verbatim quote from the RFC to confirm the hierarchy candidate.\nwhy keep: This unresolved question directly determines whether candidate #2's hierarchy claim survives or must be weakened.\nunfinished: Waiting on research fetch of RFC 9110 §9.2.","handle":"B3","kind":"scratch","ref":"sha256:ee88c0ed342a6146c00d6beaf84e02a75c18c267ccb1fdf3ea9bb918d261430d"}],"output_target":"answer","problem_ref":"question-4dd976434b71f6e815508e52e995af6b","problem_text":"Deterministic replay in an append-only reasoning system depends on retried operations being safe to repeat. Drawing on published definitions, what precisely distinguishes an idempotent operation from a safe one, and which of the two properties does an append-only event log actually require of its writers to survive at-least-once delivery? You may propose directed research fetches of specific https pages; this run's frozen domain allowlist permits en.wikipedia.org and www.rfc-editor.org (RFC 9110 defines both terms). Fetched pages become citable evidence blocks.","process_observations":[{"formal_seq":281,"id":"sha256:e393d85e31c7dd9dd711ffb2f87d35b2f08a99c519723bcc424115ec81dd57aa","observation_kind":"acceptance","related_refs":[],"schema":"bridge.process-observation.v1","statement":"At 