TP-semantics-local and does not transfer cleanly to event-log writer semantics. The append-only log does not require 'safe' or 'idempotent' in the RFC sense at all; it requires a stronger, log-specific property: deterministic-replay idempotence, where the replay of a sequence of operations reproduces the same final state regardless of delivery multiplicity, which is neither pure safety nor pure idempotence.\nMECHANISM: RFC 9110 defines idempotence per-method: 'several identical requests have the same effect as one.' But an event log operates on sequences with causality (ordering, dependencies). The required property is that the composition of retried appends is equivalent to a single canonical sequence — a property of the aggregate, not of individual operations. The mechanism is content-addressing or operation-ID dedup","handle":"A3","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"03999d039dc162aaf7537c680eece5767660079c4b661c53d5b5614f720b6556"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 281, artifact 03999d039dc162aaf7537c680eece5767660079c4b661c53d5b5614f720b6556 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P3","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:7a11d6767a4c9ddf2bf87582a1aa1d4fcab2a96f9489a9f82a9b657a072e83e7"},{"excerpt":"[619b760e99d7] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: The append-only event log requires safe operations from its readers and idempotent operations from its writers: the asymmetry is structural. Writers mutate (so not safe) but must be repeatable (idempotent); readers must not mutate (safe) and therefore are trivially idempotent.\nMECHANISM: At-least-once delivery applies to both read and write paths in distributed logs. On the write path, idempotence via deduplication keys is the minimal requirement because safety would forbid writes. On the read path, safety suffices and implies idempotence for free. The causal mechanism is the split of the log protocol into a mutation channel (writer→log, idempotent) and an observation channel (reader←log, safe). This maps to RFC 9110's separation of methods into those that change state (PUT, POST, DELETE) and those that don't (GET, HEAD, OPTIONS).","handle":"A4","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"619b760e99d71edad675228b3450195231353faa8fb263a84b746a2073ca1b68"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 281, artifact 619b760e99d71edad675228b3450195231353faa8fb263a84b746a2073ca1b68 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P4","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:16af3e65c243b44a4cd1b1ace402f340139b17d66948a4055e63aba424b4d2aa"},{"excerpt":"[748e0b32303a] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: Per RFC 9110, a safe method is one whose semantics are essentially read-only (no server state change is intended or expected), while an idempotent method is one where multiple identical requests have the same intended server effect as a single request. The distinction is that safe forbids side effects entirely, whereas idempotent permits side effects but requires them to converge. An append-only event log with at-least-once delivery requires idempotence of its writers — not safety — because appending an event is inherently state-changing (not read-only), yet a retried append must not duplicate the event.\nMECHANISM: Safety constrains the effect class (no mutation). Idempotence constrains the effect's behavior under repetition (mutation is allowed but must be invariant under duplication). In an append-only log, writers mutate state (append), so safe","handle":"A5","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"748e0b32303ad955da49166af6f436a6b8b6f52d58e7550d08cc608cb5100dde"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 281, artifact 748e0b32303ad955da49166af6f436a6b8b6f52d58e7550d08cc608cb5100dde had formal status accepted.","handle":"P5","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:8ab640dbdf573764a92789a02554ce4c7cb66d896dd403ad01053b97f25c09ea"},{"excerpt":"[08240ac1f383] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: The append-only event log does not require idempotence in the RFC 9110 sense at all. RFC 9110 idempotence is a property of HTTP request methods defined over 'intended effe