equired property is that the composition of retried appends is equivalent to a single canonical sequence — a property of the aggregate, not of individual operations. The mechanism is content-addressing or operation-ID dedup\n[619b760e99d7] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: The append-only event log requires safe operations from its readers and idempotent operations from its writers: the asymmetry is structural. Writers mutate (so not safe) but must be repeatable (idempotent); readers must not mutate (safe) and therefore are trivially idempotent.\nMECHANISM: At-least-once delivery applies to both read and write paths in distributed logs. On the write path, idempotence via deduplication keys is the minimal requirement because safety would forbid writes. On the read path, safety suffices and implies idempotence for free. The causal mechanism is the split of the log protocol into a mutation channel (writer→log, idempotent) and an observation channel (reader←log, safe). This maps to RFC 9110's separation of methods into those that change state (PUT, POST, DELETE) and those that don't (GET, HEAD, OPTIONS).\n[748e0b32303a] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: Per RFC 9110, a safe method is one whose semantics are essentially read-only (no server state change is intended or expected), while an idempotent method is one where multiple identical requests have the same intended server effect as a single request. The distinction is that safe forbids side effects entirely, whereas idempotent permits side effects but requires them to converge. An append-only event log with at-least-once delivery requires idempotence of its writers — not safety — because appending an event is inherently state-changing (not read-only), yet a retried append must not duplicate the event.\nMECHANISM: Safety constrains the effect class (no mutation). Idempotence constrains the effect's behavior under repetition (mutation is allowed but must be invariant under duplication). In an append-only log, writers mutate state (ap