40a33f","claim":"Idempotent and safe are distinct: safe means read-only (no server state change), while idempotent means state may change but identical repeated requests yield the same result as a single one. An append-only event log surviving at-least-once delivery requires writers to be idempotent, not safe, because writers must modify state yet retries must not compound.","lineage":{"dependence_refs":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mention_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672"],"source_refs":[],"trace_refs":[],"warrant_refs":[]},"mechanism":"At-least-once delivery retries writes that may have already succeeded. If the write were merely safe it could not record an event at all (safe = no state mutation). If the write were non-idempotent, each retry would append a duplicate event, corrupting replay determinism. The causal mechanism is deduplication: the writer attaches a stable operation key (or sequence number) so the log's append path can detect a replayed entry and discard it, collapsing the effect of N retries to the effect of one. This transforms a raw append (neither safe nor idempotent) into an effectively idempotent operation. RFC 9110 separates the two precisely along this axis: safe methods are cacheable/prefetchable because they have no side effects; idempotent methods (PUT, DELETE) have side effects but are repeatable.","overturn_conditions":[],"rendered_text":"[6c4abc40a33f] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: Idempotent and safe are distinct: safe means read-only (no server state change), while idempotent means state may change but identical repeated requests yield the same result as a single one. An append-only event log surviving at-least-once delivery requires writers to be idempotent, not safe, because writers must modify state yet retries must not compound.\nMECHANISM: At-least-once delivery retries writes that may have already succeeded. If the write were merely safe it could not record an event at all (safe = no state mutation). If the write were non-idempotent, each retry would append a duplicate event, corrupting replay determinism. The causal mechanism is deduplication: the writer attaches a stable operation key (or sequence number) so the log's append path can detect a replayed entry and discard it, collapsing the effect of N retries to the e","school":"school-0"},{"addressed_problem_refs":["question-4dd976434b71f6e815508e52e995af6b"],"artifact_ref":"7e25700ea5020e50f6bb7accdf036b990c66f7155e2c517d545a95d73338006f","citation_id":"7e25700ea502","claim":"Safe is a strictly stronger property than idempotent in the sense that all safe methods are idempotent (repeating a no-op yields a no-op), but the converse fails. An append-only event log needs the weaker property — idempotent writers — because it must tolerate side effects (writing events) while bounding them under retry.","lineage":{"dependence_refs":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mention_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81"],"source_refs":[],"trace_refs":[],"warrant_refs":[]},"mechanism":"RFC 9110 classifies safe methods as idempotent because a read-only operation repeated produces no additional state change. The hierarchy is: safe ⇒ idempotent, but idempotent ⇏ safe (PUT is idempotent but not safe). The log's writers occupy the idempotent-but-not-safe cell: they mutate the log, but their mutation is repeatable. The mechanism is conditional append keyed by a content hash or client-side idempotency token; the log stores a fingerprint of prior writes and rejects duplicates at the append boundary. This is idempotence engineered into an unsafe (state-mutating) operation.","overturn_conditions":[],"rendered_text":"[7e25700ea502] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: Safe is a strictly stronger property than idempotent in the sense that all safe methods are idempotent (repeating a no-op yields a no-op), but the converse fails. An append-only event log needs the weaker property — idempotent writers — because it must tolerate side effects (writing events) while bounding them under retry.\nMECHANISM: RFC 9110 classifies safe methods as idempotent because a read-only operation repeated produces no additional state change. The hierarchy is: safe ⇒ idempotent, but idempotent ⇏ safe (PUT is idempotent but not safe). The log's writers occupy the idempotent-but-not-safe cell: they mutate the log, but their mutation is repeatable. The mechanism is conditional append keyed by a content hash or client-side idempotency token; the log stores a fingerprint of prior writes and rejects duplicates at the append boundary. This is","school":"school-0"},{"addressed_problem_refs":["question-4dd976434b71f6e815508e52e995af6b"],"artifact_ref":"03999d039dc162aaf7537c680eece5767660079c4b661c53d5b5614f720b6556","citation_id":"03999d039dc1","claim":"The RFC 9110 distinction is HTTP-semantics-local and does not transfer cleanly to event-log writer semantics. The append-only log does not require 'safe' or 'idempotent' in the RFC sense at all; it requires a stronger, log-specific property: deterministic-replay idempotence, where the replay of a sequence of operations reproduces the same final state regardless of delivery multiplicity, which is neither pure safety nor pure idempotence.","lineage":{"dependence_refs":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mention_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81"],"source_refs":[],"trace_refs":[],"warrant_refs":[]},"mechanism":"RFC 9110 defines idempotence per-method: 'several identical requests have the same effect as one.' But an event log operates on sequences with causality (ordering, dependencies). The required property is that the composition of retried appends is equivalent to a single canonical sequence — a property of the aggregate, not of individual operations. The mechanism is content-addressing or operation-ID deduplication over the entire write history, which subsumes per-operation idempotence. Calling it 'idempotent' (RFC sense) is a useful approximation but technically conflates per-method semantics with sequence semantics.","overturn_conditions":[],"rendered_text":"[03999d039dc1] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: The RFC 9110 distinction is HTTP-semantics-local and does not transfer cleanly to event-log writer semantics. The append-only log does not require 'safe' or 'idempotent' in the RFC sense at all; it requires a stronger, log-specific property: deterministic-replay idempotence, where the replay of a sequence of operations reproduces the same final state regardless of delivery multiplicity, which is neither pure safety nor pure idempotence.\nMECHANISM: RFC 9110 defines idempotence per-method: 'several identical requests have the same effect as one.' But an event log operates on sequences with causality (ordering, dependencies). The required property is that the composition of retried appends is equivalent to a single canonical sequence — a property of the aggregate, not of individual operations. The mechanism is content-addressing or operation-ID dedup","school":"school-0"},{"addressed_problem_refs":["question-4dd976434b71f6e815508e52e995af6b"],"artifact_ref":"619b760e99d71edad675228b3450195231353faa8fb263a84b746a2073ca1b68","citation_id":"619b760e99d7","claim":"The append-only event log requires safe operations from its readers and idempotent operations from its writers: the asymmetry is structural. Writers mutate (so not safe) but must be repeatable (idempotent); readers must not mutate (safe) and therefore are trivially idempotent.","lineage":{"dependence_refs":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mention_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"source_refs":[],"trace_refs":[],"warrant_refs":[]},"mechanism":"At-least-once delivery applies to both read and write paths in distributed logs. On the write path, idempotence via deduplication keys is the minimal requirement because safety would forbid writes. On the read path, safety suffices and implies idempotence for free. The causal mechanism is the split of the log protocol into a mutation channel (writer→log, idempotent) and an observation channel (reader←log, safe). This maps to RFC 9110's separation of methods into those that change state (PUT, POST, DELETE) and those that don't (GET, HEAD, OPTIONS).","overturn_conditions":[],"rendered_text":"[619b760e99d7] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: The append-only event log requires safe operations from its readers and idempotent operations from its writers: the asymmetry is structural. Writers mutate (so not safe) but must be repeatable (idempotent); readers must not mutate (safe) and therefore are trivially idempotent.\nMECHANISM: At-least-once delivery applies to both read and write paths in distributed logs. On the write path, idempotence via deduplication keys is the minimal requirement because safety would forbid writes. On the read path, safety suffices and implies idempotence for free. The causal mechanism is the split of the log protocol into a mutation channel (writer→log, idempotent) and an observation channel (reader←log, safe). This maps to RFC 9110's separation of methods into those that change state (PUT, POST, DELETE) and those that don't (GET, HEAD, OPTIONS).","school":"school-0"},{"addressed_problem_refs":["question-4dd976434b71f6e815508e52e995af6b"],"artifact_ref":"748e0b32303ad955da49166af6f436a6b8b6f52d58e7550d08cc608cb5100dde","citation_id":"748e0b32303a","claim":"Per RFC 9110, a safe method is one whose semantics are essentially read-only (no server state change is intended or expected), while an idempotent method is one where multiple identical requests have the same intended server effect as a single request. The distinction is that safe forbids side effects entirely, whereas idempotent permits side effects but requires them to converge. An append-only event log with at-least-once delivery requires idempotence of its writers — not safety — because appending an event is inherently state-changing (not read-only), yet a retried append must not duplicate the event.","lineage":{"dependence_refs":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mention_refs":["6c4abc40a33fc6262d6f149079f620d6d42a8edafc3222cc8ce003e69e2b60e8","7e25700ea5020e50f6bb7accdf036b990c66f7155e2c517d545a95d73338006f","c56df74d24916e1985225e7e3720371306f0606fa692793c3519840235fd4b7a"],"source_refs":[],"trace_refs":[],"warrant_refs":[]},"mechanism":"Safety constrains the effect class (no mutation). Idempotence constrains the effect's behavior under repetition (mutation is allowed but must be invariant under duplication). In an append-only log, writers mutate state (append), so safety is impossible; but at-least-once delivery guarantees retries, so without idempotence each retry would append a duplicate, corrupting the log. Idempotent writers — typically via client-assigned sequence numbers or idempotency keys — ensure the second append of the same logical event is a no-op.","overturn_conditions":[],"rendered_text":"[748e0b32303a] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: Per RFC 9110, a safe method is one whose semantics are essentially read-only (no server state change is intended or expected), while an idempotent method is one where multiple identical requests have the same intended server effect as a single request. The distinction is that safe forbids side effects entirely, whereas idempotent permits side effects but requires them to converge. An append-only event log with at-least-once delivery requires idempotence of its writers — not safety — because appending an event is inherently state-changing (not read-only), yet a retried append must not duplicate the event.\nMECHANISM: Safety constrains the effect class (no mutation). Idempotence constrains the effect's behavior under repetition (mutation is allowed but must be invariant under duplication). In an append-only log, writers mutate state (append), so safe","school":"school-3"},{"addressed_problem_refs":["question-4dd976434b71f6e815508e52e995af6b"],"artifact_ref":"08240ac1f383f0071c84f068164f72713434b44a727553dbde5fcf445d82ddd5","citation_id":"08240ac1f383","claim":"The append-only event log does not require idempotence in the RFC 9110 sense at all. RFC 9110 idempotence is a property of HTTP request methods defined over 'intended effect on the server,' which is a semantic, declarative notion. The event log requires operational deduplication — a mechanical guarantee that duplicate physical writes are collapsed — which is a distinct and stronger property. Conflating the two obscures the design problem.","lineage":{"dependence_refs":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mention_refs":["03999d039dc162aaf7537c680eece5767660079c4b661c53d5b5614f720b6556"],"source_refs":[],"trace_refs":[],"warrant_refs":[]},"mechanism":"RFC 9110 idempotence says 'the intended effect of N identical requests equals the effect of 1.' This is a claim about the method's defined semantics, not a protocol-level mechanism. An event-log writer needs a concrete deduplication mechanism (e.g., monotonically increasing sequence numbers, content-addressed hashing, or client-assigned idempotency keys) to detect and suppress duplicates. This is an operational invariant, not an HTTP semantic classification. The two notions share the word 'idempotent' but refer to different abstraction layers.","overturn_conditions":[],"rendered_text":"[08240ac1f383] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: The append-only event log does not require idempotence in the RFC 9110 sense at all. RFC 9110 idempotence is a property of HTTP request methods defined over 'intended effect on the server,' which is a semantic, declarative notion. The event log requires operational deduplication — a mechanical guarantee that duplicate physical writes are collapsed — which is a distinct and stronger property. Conflating the two obscures the design problem.\nMECHANISM: RFC 9110 idempotence says 'the intended effect of N identical requests equals the effect of 1.' This is a claim about the method's defined semantics, not a protocol-level mechanism. An event-log writer needs a concrete deduplication mechanism (e.g., monotonically increasing sequence numbers, content-addressed hashing, or client-assigned idempotency keys) to detect and suppress duplicates. This is an op","school":"school-3"},{"addressed_problem_refs":["question-4dd976434b71f6e815508e52e995af6b"],"artifact_ref":"cded8b977c502922870c0972bda9fcaf7458e11559be202d588bfcd3c98253f3","citation_id":"cded8b977c50","claim":"RFC 9110 establishes that safe methods are a proper subset of idempotent methods: a safe method produces no side effects, so repeating it trivially yields the same (null) effect, satisfying idempotence. The converse fails: PUT and DELETE are idempotent but not safe. An append-only event log with at-least-once delivery requires idempotence — the weaker, more general property — because writers must mutate state (violating safety) but must not duplicate events under retry (requiring idempotence). Requiring safety would be self-contradictory for a write operation.","lineage":{"dependence_refs":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mention_refs":["7e25700ea5020e50f6bb7accdf036b990c66f7155e2c517d545a95d73338006f","c56df74d24916e1985225e7e3720371306f0606fa692793c3519840235fd4b7a"],"source_refs":[],"trace_refs":[],"warrant_refs":[]},"mechanism":"The subset relation follows from the definitions: safety means 'no intended state change' (RFC 9110 §9.2.1), and idempotence means 'same intended effect for N requests as for 1' (§9.2.2). If the effect of one request is 'no state change,' then the effect of N requests is also 'no state change,' so safe ⟹ idempotent. The event-log writer's append is a mutation (not safe) but must be designed to converge under repetition (idempotent). This places it squarely in the idempotent-but-not-safe region, alongside HTTP PUT.","overturn_conditions":[],"rendered_text":"[cded8b977c50] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: RFC 9110 establishes that safe methods are a proper subset of idempotent methods: a safe method produces no side effects, so repeating it trivially yields the same (null) effect, satisfying idempotence. The converse fails: PUT and DELETE are idempotent but not safe. An append-only event log with at-least-once delivery requires idempotence — the weaker, more general property — because writers must mutate state (violating safety) but must not duplicate events under retry (requiring idempotence). Requiring safety would be self-contradictory for a write operation.\nMECHANISM: The subset relation follows from the definitions: safety means 'no intended state change' (RFC 9110 §9.2.1), and idempotence means 'same intended effect for N requests as for 1' (§9.2.2). If the effect of one request is 'no state change,' then the effect of N requests is also 'no ","school":"school-3"},{"addressed_problem_refs":["question-4dd976434b71f6e815508e52e995af6b"],"artifact_ref":"8e394f49e7f08af01a5485db4afa96c57bd95d7bd742d77f6a1b22f4c002c8ef","citation_id":"8e394f49e7f0","claim":"The critical distinction is not merely 'safe = read-only, idempotent = convergent under repetition,' but rather that RFC 9110 safety carries a client-expectation dimension: a safe method is one where 'the client does not request, and does not expect, any state change.' Idempotence carries no such expectation — the client expects a state change but expects it to be invariant under duplication. For the event log, this client-expectation dimension is decisive: writers always expect and request a state change (append), so safety is categorically impossible regardless of deduplication strategy. The log requires idempotence because writers expect mutation but must tolerate delivery duplication.","lineage":{"dependence_refs":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mention_refs":["c56df74d24916e1985225e7e3720371306f0606fa692793c3519840235fd4b7a"],"source_refs":[],"trace_refs":[],"warrant_refs":[]},"mechanism":"RFC 9110 §9.2.1 defines safety as not merely 'no side effect' but as a contract involving client intent: the client does not expect state change. This is stronger than a purely server-side 'no mutation' property. A writer to an append-only log, by definition, requests a state change (the append), so even if deduplication makes the second attempt a no-op, the writer's first attempt is a mutation request, violating the client-expectation component of safety. Idempotence has no such client-expectation constraint — it only constrains the server's response to repetition.","overturn_conditions":[],"rendered_text":"[8e394f49e7f0] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: The critical distinction is not merely 'safe = read-only, idempotent = convergent under repetition,' but rather that RFC 9110 safety carries a client-expectation dimension: a safe method is one where 'the client does not request, and does not expect, any state change.' Idempotence carries no such expectation — the client expects a state change but expects it to be invariant under duplication. For the event log, this client-expectation dimension is decisive: writers always expect and request a state change (append), so safety is categorically impossible regardless of deduplication strategy. The log requires idempotence because writers expect mutation but must tolerate delivery duplication.\nMECHANISM: RFC 9110 §9.2.1 defines safety as not merely 'no side effect' but as a contract involving client intent: the client does not expect state change. This","school":"school-3"},{"addressed_problem_refs":["question-4dd976434b71f6e815508e52e995af6b"],"artifact_ref":"5d8c3d571ed7d5905c4f583889e38ae5035152dee6b84f0e1841785f089e636b","citation_id":"5d8c3d571ed7","claim":"An append-only event log actually requires a property stronger than RFC 9110 idempotence: it requires write-ordered idempotence, where not only must duplicate writes be suppressed, but the ordering of non-duplicate writes must be preserved. RFC 9110 idempotence is order-insensitive (PUT A then PUT A then PUT B yields the same state as PUT A then PUT B). But an event log with at-least-once delivery needs order-preserving deduplication: event A, event B, retry of event A must yield [A, B], not [A, B, A]. This ordering constraint is absent from RFC 9110's definition and represents a strictly stronger requirement.","lineage":{"dependence_refs":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mention_refs":["03999d039dc162aaf7537c680eece5767660079c4b661c53d5b5614f720b6556"],"source_refs":[],"trace_refs":[],"warrant_refs":[]},"mechanism":"RFC 9110 idempotence for PUT says N identical requests yield the same final state as 1 — it says nothing about interleaving with other requests. In an event log, events are ordered, and a retry of an earlier event arriving after later events have been appended must not produce a duplicate in the wrong position. This requires the log to track which logical events have already been appended (e.g., by sequence number) and suppress late-arriving retries, preserving the original order. This is deduplication with ordering, a compound property not captured by either 'safe' or 'idempotent' alone.","overturn_conditions":[],"rendered_text":"[5d8c3d571ed7] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: An append-only event log actually requires a property stronger than RFC 9110 idempotence: it requires write-ordered idempotence, where not only must duplicate writes be suppressed, but the ordering of non-duplicate writes must be preserved. RFC 9110 idempotence is order-insensitive (PUT A then PUT A then PUT B yields the same state as PUT A then PUT B). But an event log with at-least-once delivery needs order-preserving deduplication: event A, event B, retry of event A must yield [A, B], not [A, B, A]. This ordering constraint is absent from RFC 9110's definition and represents a strictly stronger requirement.\nMECHANISM: RFC 9110 idempotence for PUT says N identical requests yield the same final state as 1 — it says nothing about interleaving with other requests. In an event log, events are ordered, and a retry of an earlier event arriving after l","school":"school-3"},{"addressed_problem_refs":["question-4dd976434b71f6e815508e52e995af6b"],"artifact_ref":"519095e5cfa6b4a47054b6b640cd8c977dfbaa5974fddf4c0b4d03f3335f6126","citation_id":"519095e5cfa6","claim":"The distinction between safe and idempotent in RFC 9110 is fundamentally about the presence of side effects: safe methods have none (they are read-only operations that do not modify any server resource), while idempotent methods may have side effects but guarantee that the side effects of repeated identical requests equal those of a single request. For an append-only event log, the required property is idempotence. The log cannot require safety because appending is inherently a mutation. But it must require idempotence because at-least-once delivery means the writer's append request may be received and processed multiple times, and the log must remain correct — containing each logical event exactly once regardless of how many times the delivery layer retries.","lineage":{"dependence_refs":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mention_refs":["6c4abc40a33fc6262d6f149079f620d6d42a8edafc3222cc8ce003e69e2b60e8","c56df74d24916e1985225e7e3720371306f0606fa692793c3519840235fd4b7a"],"source_refs":[],"trace_refs":[],"warrant_refs":[]},"mechanism":"Safe = no side effects (read-only). Idempotent = side effects allowed but invariant under repetition. At-least-once delivery means the network may deliver the same append request multiple times. If the append operation is not idempotent, each redelivery creates a new duplicate event, corrupting the log. If it is idempotent (via deduplication keys or sequence numbering), redelivery is absorbed as a no-op. Safety would be self-defeating: it would mean the append does nothing, making the log useless.","overturn_conditions":[],"rendered_text":"[519095e5cfa6] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: The distinction between safe and idempotent in RFC 9110 is fundamentally about the presence of side effects: safe methods have none (they are read-only operations that do not modify any server resource), while idempotent methods may have side effects but guarantee that the side effects of repeated identical requests equal those of a single request. For an append-only event log, the required property is idempotence. The log cannot require safety because appending is inherently a mutation. But it must require idempotence because at-least-once delivery means the writer's append request may be received and processed multiple times, and the log must remain correct — containing each logical event exactly once regardless of how many times the delivery layer retries.\nMECHANISM: Safe = no side effects (read-only). Idempotent = side effects allowed but inva","school":"school-3"}]},"formal_seq":281,"manifest_digest":"5f25d072f10846a9119812cff8df1d80b34742e2cb2e97bcd7b739459205fe87","problem_id":"question-4dd976434b71f6e815508e52e995af6b","schema":"bridge.execution-snapshot.v1","source_run_digest":null,"source_terminal_commitment_ref":"sha256:730a164c6d14350df61db470ae16a0af4ac5d10e254b01549ad12861fc2acfc4","target":"answer","workflow_policy":{"composer_role":"thesis","composition_contract_version":"v2","grounding_review":false,"ledger_contract_version":"v3","ledger_role":"summarizer","max_grounding_repair_attempts":0,"max_ledger_amendments":1,"reviewer_role":"judge"}}